Description: Provide org.freedesktop.a11y.KeyboardMonitor for screen readers
 Orca needs to hear every key and to keep its own commands (the Orca key,
 Insert, keypad Insert or Caps Lock, together with another key) away from
 the focused application. In the X11 session it gets this through AT-SPI
 and X key grabs. In the sway session it found no keyboard monitor on the
 session bus, so its commands leaked into the focused application (typed
 into a browser page, for example) and it could not hear keys in
 applications that do not forward them.
 .
 sway now owns org.freedesktop.a11y.Manager on the session bus and
 provides the org.freedesktop.a11y.KeyboardMonitor interface at
 /org/freedesktop/a11y/Manager, as mutter, KWin, niri and cosmic-comp do.
 Only the owner of org.gnome.Orca.KeyboardMonitor may use it; other
 callers get AccessDenied. KeyEvent signals go to the interested client
 only. WatchKeyboard reports keys; GrabKeyboard and SetKeyGrabs also keep
 them from applications and bindings. A release goes where its press went,
 a client told about a press is told about its release, and a grabbed
 Orca key tapped twice within the repeat delay reaches the application.
 A client's state ends when its bus connection closes. Without a session
 bus sway runs as before.
 .
 The hook sits in sway_keyboard_handle_key(), so it sees keys after the
 keyboard accessibility options of 07-keyboard-accessibility.patch: slow
 keys and bounce keys filter first, and the screen reader hears the keys
 that are actually typed, as in GNOME.
